# DTF tier manifest: gcs-error-url-redaction (artifact-keeper#5093, PR #5094)
# Profile-set: gcs-emulator / single (the profile of gcs-custom-endpoint, which is
# only an upload/download round trip and never sees a failing storage request).
#
# THE BUG: a reqwest transport error stringifies with the request URL, which names
# the endpoint host, the bucket and the object key. The GCS backend copied that
# text into its storage errors, so a refused connection or an unresolvable
# GCS_ENDPOINT host showed all three in the unauthenticated /health and /healthz
# storage message and in API error bodies such as the OCI blob-upload 500.
#
# METHOD: the oracle stops the emulator mid-run, so every GCS request fails in
# transport, then reads what a client sees.
#
#   (FIX) /health and /healthz report storage unhealthy, and their bodies name no
#         URL, no endpoint host or port, no bucket; a failing OCI blob upload's
#         body names none of those and no object key.
#         RED on 1.11.10: each of those bodies repeats the request URL.
#   (CONTROL) both images: with the emulator up, storage is healthy and a blob
#         upload succeeds; with it down the health check really does report the
#         failure, so the absence of a URL is not the absence of a message. A
#         failing Maven upload and download name no URL either, which already held
#         on 1.11.10: they guard the same text on another route, they do not
#         discriminate the fix.
#
# NOT PROVEN HERE: fake-gcs-server runs in the emulator's static-token mode, so the
# service-account and metadata-server token fetches (also redacted by the fix) are
# not exercised. Real Google Cloud Storage is not used. The S3 and Azure sites the
# same PR trimmed are unit-tested in artifact-keeper only.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: not under test.
PROFILES="storage.gcs-emulator"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
